# 9. Input Validation

Every tool validates:

- Required fields
- Data types
- Supported actions
- Existing Order IDs

Invalid requests return structured errors instead of runtime failures.

---

# 10. Safety Considerations

The MCP server intentionally limits what actions can be executed.

Safety measures include:

- No arbitrary code execution.
- No unrestricted database access.
- Fixed action whitelist.
- Synthetic datasets only.
- Read-only investigation tools by default.
- Action execution restricted to predefined operations.

These constraints reduce the likelihood of unintended behavior while keeping demonstrations deterministic.

---

# 11. Error Response Format

All tools return predictable error structures.

Example

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "ORDER_NOT_FOUND",
    "message": "No order exists with ID ORD-999."
  }
}
```

This enables the LLM to generate clear and consistent user-facing explanations.

---

# 12. Extensibility

The MCP architecture is designed to support additional operational capabilities.

Future tools may include:

- investigate_refund
- investigate_return
- investigate_inventory
- investigate_carrier_delay
- notify_customer
- escalate_incident

Adding new tools does not require changes to existing tool contracts.

---

# 13. Design Decisions

| Decision | Reason |
|-----------|--------|
| Business-oriented tools | Better alignment with operational workflows |
| Structured outputs | Easier for LLM reasoning |
| Service layer | Keeps tools lightweight |
| Whitelisted actions | Improves safety |
| Synthetic data | Reproducible demonstrations |
| Validation at tool boundary | Prevents invalid requests |

---

# 14. Summary

The MCP server is intentionally designed as the operational intelligence layer of the Commerce Operations Copilot.

Instead of exposing backend resources directly, it provides high-level business capabilities that allow AI models to investigate operational issues, recommend appropriate actions, and execute safe resolutions.

This design keeps the MCP central to the product while maintaining clear separation between AI reasoning, business logic, and data access.